{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01MZERfKD9HwMtges3mv8jbK","type":"tool_result","content":"{\"result\":\"{\\\"success\\\": true, \\\"data\\\": {\\\"path\\\": \\\"/home/ubuntu/.agent-ml-research-idea_gen_0519/projects/nlp_variance_gtheory/decisions.yaml\\\", \\\"content\\\": \\\"_next_id: 6\\\\ndecisions:\\\\n- id: D001\\\\n  date: 2026-05-20 18:57 UTC\\\\n  summary: MVP PASS — G-theory 方差分解在 LLM 评估中可行，item 方差主导（58.4%），separability 在聚合层面成立，进入\\\\n    research 阶段\\\\n  rationale: 5,400 次推理完成，5 个方差分量 ≥5% 且 CI 不含零，pipeline 端到端可运行。核心发现：(1) item 选择是评估方差最大来源；(2)\\\\n    precision/temperature/prompt 效应是 item-specific 的；(3) 直接的 infrastructure×methodology\\\\n    交互可忽略（<0.02%）。float32 引擎混淆需全研究阶段解决但不影响 MVP 结论。\\\\n  related_experiments: []\\\\n  depends_on: []\\\\n- id: D002\\\\n  date: 2026-05-20 19:12 UTC\\\\n  summary: 采用 dual-metric (binary correct + text exact match) 方差分解作为论文核心方法论，Claim\\\\n    3 改为 metric-conditional reproducibility budget\\\\n  rationale: MVP 双变量分析揭示截然不同的方差结构：binary correct 下 item_id 主导 (58.4%, G=0.357)；text\\\\n    exact match 下 prompt×item 主导 (37.5%, G=0.975)。单一 response variable 无法完整描述评估可靠性。Dual-metric\\\\n    对比是 G-theory 框架的天然优势——\\\\\\\"同一实验在不同衡量标准下可靠性完全不同\\\\\\\"是比单一 budget 更有力的发现。precision:temperature\\\\n    交互在 text metric 下显著 (1.3%, CI 不含零)，证明 separability 在细粒度下不完全成立。\\\\n  related_experiments: []\\\\n  depends_on:\\\\n  - D001\\\\n- id: D003\\\\n  date: 2026-05-20 22:20 UTC\\\\n  summary: Dream 审稿后调整 framing：infrastructure crossing 降级为 confirmatory，cross-benchmark\\\\n    gradient 升级为 primary novelty，model-as-facet 加入分析计划\\\\n  rationale: Dream full review (dream_20260520T190053) 三档评分：Best 3/6, Median 4/6,\\\\n    Worst 3/6。三档一致暴露 9 个结构性问题：(1) bibliography 严重不足（需 25+ refs）; (2) model 未作为 G-theory\\\\n    facet（inflates item_id）; (3) 无 bootstrap CIs; (4) 零 figures; (5) infrastructure\\\\n    crossing 为 confirmatory null 非 breakthrough; (6) item dominance 心理测量学预期，oversold;\\\\n    (7) \\\\\\\"fully crossed\\\\\\\" 过度声称; (8) \\\\\\\"diagnostic tool\\\\\\\" 无实质; (9) fine-tuning 用 RoBERTa\\\\n    距离太远。全部为 framing/分析问题，不影响实验设计——P0 实验不变，但 framing 需大改，exp-004 分析内容扩展。需 re-dream\\\\n    验证后批准 P0。\\\\n  related_experiments:\\\\n  - exp_id: exp-001\\\\n    role: unchanged\\\\n    result: design not affected by framing changes\\\\n  - exp_id: exp-004\\\\n    role: expanded\\\\n    result: added model-as-facet, bootstrap CIs, higher-order interactions, IRT comparison\\\\n  depends_on:\\\\n  - D001\\\\n  - D002\\\\n- id: D004\\\\n  date: 2026-05-21 00:08 UTC\\\\n  summary: 'Re-dream #2 通过质量门控（Best 4/6, Median 4/6），批准 exp-001 启动'\\\\n  rationale: 'Dream #2 评分：Best 4/6 (Weak Accept), Median 4/6 (Weak Accept), Worst\\\\n    3/6 (Weak Reject)。Best ≥ weak accept 且 Median ≥ borderline，满足 post-MVP kickoff\\\\n    Step 5 的批准条件。相比 Dream #1 (3/4/3)，Best 从 3→4 提升，结构性问题已解决。剩余问题均为分析/呈现层面：(1) 零 figures\\\\n    — 论文写作阶段处理; (2) D-study 优化过程需文档化 — exp-004; (3) Bootstrap 重采样独立性问题 — 加 jackknife\\\\n    对比到 exp-004; (4) G 系数解读需区分 item-level 与 benchmark-level — 论文 Discussion; (5) Cross-benchmark\\\\n    设计不对称需 sensitivity analysis — exp-004; (6) IRT 对比 — 已纳入 exp-004; (7) Exp 5/6/7\\\\n    建议移至 appendix — 论文写作。所有剩余问题不影响 exp-001 实验设计。'\\\\n  related_experiments:\\\\n  - exp_id: exp-001\\\\n    role: approved\\\\n    result: re-dream passes, proceed with full-scale experiment\\\\n  depends_on:\\\\n  - D003\\\\n- id: D005\\\\n  date: 2026-05-21 02:26 UTC\\\\n  summary: Gemma2 fp16 不可用 — cross-model 分析用 bf16+fp32 平衡设计，fp16 仅用于 within-model\\\\n  rationale: vLLM 报告 Gemma2 架构不支持 fp16（numerical instability）。bf16 vs fp16 差异 <0.1pp（Llama\\\\n    数据），precision 信号集中在 fp32 vs 其他（10.7pp）。cross-model 用 2 precisions 不损失 power，fp16\\\\n    数据在 Llama/Mistral/Qwen within-model 分析中保留。\\\\n  related_experiments:\\\\n  - exp_id: exp-001\\\\n    role: affected\\\\n    result: Gemma fp16 conditions marked N/A, cross-model analysis scoped to bf16+fp32\\\\n  depends_on:\\\\n  - D004\\\\n\\\", \\\"total_chars\\\": 3318, \\\"truncated\\\": false}, \\\"error\\\": null, \\\"metadata\\\": {}}\"}"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":"toolu_01139V1TFyXMbhEn8xDmZP2o","session_id":"db6a3d8a-492b-4ec1-b68e-c01acc7fc375","uuid":"31d6a0c2-9128-4eba-a5e9-134514260a96","timestamp":"2026-05-21T04:26:01.779Z"}
{"type":"system","subtype":"task_notification","task_id":"a12b20bb301cc8a7a","tool_use_id":"toolu_01139V1TFyXMbhEn8xDmZP2o","status":"completed","output_file":"","summary":"Full paper review","usage":{"total_tokens":69847,"tool_uses":17,"duration_ms":172356},"uuid":"68c3b7a3-5d12-4107-87f0-48405a177017","session_id":"db6a3d8a-492b-4ec1-b68e-c01acc7fc375"}
{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01139V1TFyXMbhEn8xDmZP2o","type":"tool_result","content":[{"type":"text","text":"This is very revealing. The project has only completed the exp-001 Llama run (success), a pilot, an MVP, and a bf16 disentangle experiment. The Gemma run failed. The paper presents results from exp-001 through exp-008, but the review_context.yaml contains \"imagined_experiments\" with \"expected_results\" -- meaning the paper's numbers are projected/imagined, not from actual experiments.\n\nNow I have everything I need for the review.\n\n---\n\n## 1. Summary\n\nThis paper applies Generalizability Theory (G-theory) from psychometrics to LLM evaluation, decomposing observed variance in benchmark scores into contributions from items, prompt templates, random seeds, temperature, option ordering, numerical precision, and GPU type. The study crosses infrastructure and methodology facets across five benchmarks (MMLU, ARC-Challenge, HellaSwag, GSM8K, MATH), six models (four 7-9B, two 14B), and multiple scoring metrics (binary, text-exact-match, log-probability). Three main findings are reported: (1) item selection dominance is benchmark-conditional, following a monotonic gradient from knowledge retrieval (57.1% on MMLU) to reasoning (16.8% on MATH); (2) infrastructure-methodology interactions are negligible under binary scoring but not text-exact-match; (3) reproducibility budgets differ 7.1x across benchmarks, and IRT-based approaches underestimate required evaluations by 2.8-4.2x. The paper additionally reports a fine-tuning G-study showing a qualitatively different variance hierarchy where training seed rivals item selection.\n\n## 2. Strengths\n\n1. **Well-motivated research question.** The paper addresses a genuine and practically important gap: prior work isolates one or two variance sources in LLM evaluation, but no study has tested whether these factors interact in a unified factorial design. The framing around \"reproducibility budgets\" is actionable and timely.\n\n2. **Appropriate methodological framework.** G-theory is a natural fit for this problem. The distinction between G-studies (variance decomposition) and D-studies (reliability projection) is precisely what the LLM evaluation community needs. The paper clearly explains the framework and makes it accessible to an ML audience.\n\n3. **Comprehensive experimental design on paper.** The claimed scale (3.4M+ records, 8 experiments, 5 benchmarks, 6 models, 3 metrics) is impressive. The factorial structure crossing infrastructure with methodology facets is novel relative to Mesting (2026), who studied only methodology facets on a single benchmark.\n\n4. **Strong primary finding.** The cross-benchmark variance gradient (Claim 1) is genuinely novel and explanatory. The monotonic relationship (r = -0.99) between cognitive complexity and item dominance elegantly reconciles contradictory findings in the literature. The within-MATH difficulty-level analysis (Table 8) provides a compelling micro-level confirmation.\n\n5. **Metric-conditional separability is a nuanced contribution.** Rather than simply claiming separability or non-separability, the paper shows that the answer depends on the scoring metric. This is a more useful finding than a binary yes/no would be.\n\n6. **Extensive sensitivity analyses.** The paper tests robustness to estimation method (REML vs. Henderson), facet treatment (fixed vs. random), design asymmetry, bootstrap vs. jackknife CIs, and Bernoulli correction. This thoroughness addresses many potential methodological critiques proactively.\n\n7. **Practical D-study outputs.** Tables 15-16 provide concrete, benchmark-specific allocations for achieving G >= 0.90, which practitioners can directly use. The IRT comparison (Table 17) contextualizes these against existing recommendations.\n\n8. **Item resampling stability (exp-001b).** Testing three independent 200-item draws with zero overlap and finding SD = 1.4pp is an effective defense against the concern that results are artifact of a specific item sample.\n\n## 3. Weaknesses\n\n1. **CRITICAL: The paper appears to present projected/imagined results rather than completed experiments.** (Severity: **major**) The project's actual experimental state shows only exp-001 for Llama completed successfully (239,200 records), with the Gemma run having failed. Yet the paper reports results from 8 experiments totaling 3.4M+ records across 4 models, 5 benchmarks, multiple GPUs, fine-tuning, etc. The review_context.yaml file is titled \"imagined_experiments\" with \"expected_results.\" If these numbers are not from actual experimental data, this is a fundamental integrity concern. Every number in the paper beyond the Llama MMLU results may be fabricated.\n\n2. **Suspiciously clean numbers.** (Severity: **major**) The variance decomposition percentages across models in Tables 2-3 show implausibly low cross-model variability. For example, item_id ranges from 55.8-59.1% across four architecturally different models (Llama, Qwen, Mistral, Gemma), with SD = 1.4pp. Residual ranges from 16.3-18.2%. Every single variance component falls within a very narrow band across models. Real experimental data from architecturally distinct models would be expected to show more heterogeneity. The cross-benchmark gradient (Table 5) is also perfectly monotonic with suspiciously round numbers.\n\n3. **The perfectly monotonic gradient is overclaimed.** (Severity: **moderate**) The r = -0.99 correlation between \"cognitive complexity\" (which is assigned as ranks 1-5 by the authors themselves) and item_id% is circular. The authors define the ordering of cognitive complexity to match the observed item_id ordering, then report a near-perfect correlation. With only 5 data points and researcher-assigned ranks, this correlation is not statistically meaningful. A more rigorous approach would use an independent measure of cognitive complexity.\n\n4. **FP32 degradation conflated with precision effects.** (Severity: **moderate**) The paper acknowledges that fp32 shows ~8.9pp degradation due to \"V0 enforce_eager mode\" affecting the attention computation path, not numerical precision per se. Yet fp32 is included as a level of the \"precision\" facet throughout. This means the \"precision\" variance component is substantially inflated by a vLLM engine artifact. The precision:item interaction (7.0%) on MMLU is likely dominated by this confound rather than genuine precision effects. The paper acknowledges this in the accuracy section but does not adequately address how it distorts the variance decomposition.\n\n5. **Binary G-theory limitations insufficiently addressed.** (Severity: **moderate**) The paper applies standard G-theory to binary (0/1) data. While the Limitations section mentions this and cites Bernoulli correction (informative item_id = 30.7%), the main text reports uncorrected values (57.1%, 47.3%) as headline numbers. The 35% mechanical variance fraction is substantial. The corrected 30.7% is still the largest component, but using uncorrected figures as headlines is misleading.\n\n6. **\"Cognitive complexity\" ranking is ad hoc.** (Severity: **moderate**) Labeling MMLU as complexity 1, ARC as 2, HellaSwag as 3, GSM8K as 4, and MATH as 5 is not grounded in any validated cognitive taxonomy. HellaSwag (commonsense completion) arguably requires different cognitive skills than ARC (science reasoning), not necessarily \"more complex\" ones. The gradient may reflect item difficulty or answer-space characteristics rather than cognitive complexity per se.\n\n7. **Temperature as a random facet with 2-3 levels is problematic.** (Severity: **moderate**) G-theory treats facets as random samples from a universe of conditions. Temperature is a continuous parameter where 0.0, 0.3, and 0.7 are not random samples. With only 2-3 levels, variance component estimates for temperature are poorly powered. The sensitivity analysis showing <3pp change when treated as fixed partially addresses this, but the main analysis still uses the random-facet assumption.\n\n8. **No figures.** (Severity: **moderate**) The paper contains zero figures. For a paper whose core contribution is a visual gradient across benchmarks, this is a significant presentation weakness. The variance decomposition stacked bar chart is essentially required reading to communicate the cross-benchmark pattern effectively. D-study projection curves would also greatly aid comprehension.\n\n9. **Fine-tuning experiment (exp-006) is disconnected.** (Severity: **minor**) Using RoBERTa-large (encoder-only, 355M parameters) for the fine-tuning G-study is a very different regime from 7-14B decoder-only LLM inference. The finding that training seed rivals item selection is interesting but of limited relevance to the paper's main claims about LLM evaluation. The paper acknowledges this limitation but still devotes significant space to it.\n\n10. **Only two GPU types.** (Severity: **minor**) RTX PRO 6000 and A100-80GB are both NVIDIA data-center-class GPUs. The separability claim would be stronger with more diverse hardware (e.g., different generations, consumer vs. data-center, or different vendors if possible).\n\n11. **MATH benchmark uses option ordering facet despite being open-ended.** (Severity: **minor**) MATH problems are open-ended, not multiple-choice. The option ordering facet (4 systematic rotations) should not apply to MATH. The paper does not clarify how this facet was handled for MATH and GSM8K. If ordering was not varied, the design is not fully crossed for these benchmarks.\n\n## 4. Questions for Authors\n\n1. **How many of the reported experiments have been actually completed with real data, vs. projected from models or simulations?** The project files indicate only exp-001 Llama was completed. Can you confirm that all numbers in all tables come from actual experimental runs?\n\n2. **Why is cross-model variability in variance decomposition so low?** SD = 1.4pp across four architecturally distinct models seems implausibly tight. What is the expected sampling variability for these estimates?\n\n3. **How was the \"cognitive complexity\" ranking of benchmarks determined?** Was this ranking established a priori or assigned post hoc to match the observed gradient?\n\n4. **How was the option ordering facet handled for GSM8K and MATH?** These are not multiple-choice benchmarks in the traditional sense. Were answer options artificially constructed?\n\n5. **What happens to the variance decomposition if fp32 is excluded?** Given the acknowledged engine confound, what does the precision facet look like with only bf16 vs. fp16?\n\n6. **Can you provide the actual raw data or analysis scripts?** Given the concern about imagined results, reproducibility verification is essential.\n\n7. **Why does G increase from MMLU (0.36) to MATH (0.51) despite MATH having higher interaction dominance?** The paper offers an explanation but it seems counterintuitive. Can you provide a formal derivation showing this is expected?\n\n8. **What is the total record count from experiments that have actually been run?** The paper claims 3.4M+. The project logs show ~239K.\n\n## 5. Missing References or Related Work\n\n1. **Lucic et al. (2018)** -- \"Are GANs Created Equal?\" -- early systematic variance decomposition in deep learning evaluation.\n2. **Zhu et al. (2024)** -- \"PromptBench\" -- systematic prompt robustness evaluation that would contextualize the prompt sensitivity findings.\n3. **Gao et al. (2023)** -- lm-evaluation-harness -- the de facto standard evaluation framework whose defaults this work implicitly critiques. Should be cited and discussed.\n4. **Zheng et al. (2023)** -- \"Judging LLM-as-a-Judge\" -- relevant to the choice of not including judge as a facet, which Mesting does include.\n5. **Webb et al. (2006)** -- updated G-theory references beyond the 1991 primer.\n6. **Cardinet et al. (2010)** -- \"Applying Generalizability Theory Using EduG\" -- practical G-theory reference.\n\n## 6. Detailed Comments\n\n**Abstract:** The abstract is well-structured and quantitative. However, the claim \"first multi-benchmark Generalizability Theory analysis\" should be verified carefully against Mesting (2026), who may cover multiple tasks even if focused on a single benchmark.\n\n**Section 3.1 (G-theory background):** The formula for G-coefficient omits the distinction between relative and absolute error, which matters for interpretation. Clarify whether you report relative or absolute G.\n\n**Table 1 (Accuracy):** The fp32 degradation is consistently 10+ pp across all models. This needs more discussion -- is this a meaningful precision effect or purely a vLLM artifact? If the latter, including fp32 as a \"precision\" level is misleading.\n\n**Table 5 (Cross-benchmark gradient):** The sum of all components for MMLU is exactly 100.0%, but for other benchmarks it is also exactly 100.0%. This is expected for variance decomposition, but the perfect rounding across 10+ components for 5 benchmarks is suspicious.\n\n**Section 4.5 (D-study):** The D-study optimization procedure is underdocumented. What objective function was used? Was it a grid search over all facet levels? What constraints were imposed? The allocations in Table 15 appear to have been jointly optimized, but the procedure is not described.\n\n**Section 4.6 (Logprob):** The logprob analysis uses model-as-facet item_id numbers (52.3% for MMLU) rather than single-model numbers. This inconsistency with earlier sections (which report single-model figures for binary/text) is confusing.\n\n**Section 5 (Discussion):** The \"why the gradient exists\" paragraph offers a plausible mechanistic explanation (multi-step reasoning compounds sampling randomness), but this is a just-so story without empirical validation. A more compelling approach would be to show that variance components correlate with solution length or number of reasoning steps within a benchmark.\n\n**Limitations:** The paper does a good job acknowledging limitations, but the most critical one (that results may not be from real experiments) is of course not mentioned. The Bernoulli limitation is well-handled.\n\n**Bibliography:** Only 21 references for a paper of this scope. A venue like AAAI would expect 30-40 for a methods paper.\n\n## 7. Overall Assessment\n\n- **Novelty: 4/5** -- The cross-benchmark variance gradient and infrastructure-methodology separability test are genuinely novel contributions relative to Mesting (2026) and other prior work. The G-theory framework application itself is incremental over Mesting.\n\n- **Rigor: 2/5** -- The experimental rigor is severely undermined by strong evidence that most reported results are projected/imagined rather than from completed experiments. The project has completed only ~239K of the claimed 3.4M+ records. If the results are real, the sensitivity analyses and bootstrap CIs would earn a 4/5. In the current state, rigor cannot be evaluated.\n\n- **Clarity: 3/5** -- The writing is generally clear and well-organized. The absence of figures is a significant deficit. Some tables are dense and could be better presented. The distinction between single-model and model-as-facet analyses is sometimes confusing.\n\n- **Significance: 4/5** -- If the findings hold up with real data, they would be significant for the LLM evaluation community. The D-study reproducibility budgets are directly actionable. The IRT vs. G-theory comparison is valuable.\n\n- **Reproducibility: 1/5** -- No code release, no data release, and strong evidence that the experimental results themselves have not been produced yet. The project logs show 5 experiments with only 3 successful, far short of the 8 experiments claimed.\n\n- **Overall Score: 3/6 (Weak Reject)**\n\n- **Confidence: 4/5** -- I have high confidence in this assessment based on thorough review of the paper, project files, roadmap, experimental logs, and the review_context.yaml which explicitly labels experiments as \"imagined.\"\n\n- **One-line summary:** The research question is excellent and the G-theory framework is well-chosen, but the paper presents projected/imagined results as completed experiments, which is a fatal flaw; even setting that aside, the perfectly clean cross-model consistency, ad hoc cognitive complexity ranking, fp32 confound, and absence of figures weaken what could otherwise be a strong contribution."},{"type":"text","text":"agentId: a12b20bb301cc8a7a (use SendMessage with to: 'a12b20bb301cc8a7a' to continue this agent)\n<usage>total_tokens: 73611\ntool_uses: 17\nduration_ms: 172359</usage>"}]}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"db6a3d8a-492b-4ec1-b68e-c01acc7fc375","uuid":"e62ae0c7-980c-49cd-b3c9-0e5580eb98b0","timestamp":"2026-05-21T04:27:48.306Z","tool_use_result":{"status":"completed","prompt":"You are an expert ML reviewer at a top venue (acceptance rate ~25%). Review the following paper thoroughly and produce a structured review. Use a 6-point scale: 1=Strong Reject, 2=Reject, 3=Weak Reject, 4=Weak Accept, 5=Accept, 6=Strong Accept.\n\n# Paper Summary\n\n**Title**: \"Reproducibility Budgets for LLM Evaluation: A Generalizability Theory Analysis of Variance Sources Across Benchmarks, Models, and Evaluation Metrics\"\n\n**Core Contribution**: The first multi-benchmark Generalizability Theory (G-theory) analysis of LLM evaluation, crossing infrastructure factors (numerical precision, GPU type) with methodology factors (prompt template, random seed, sampling temperature, option ordering) across five benchmarks (MMLU, ARC-Challenge, HellaSwag, GSM8K, MATH), four 7-9B models, two 14B models, and two evaluation metrics.\n\n**Three Main Claims**:\n1. **Cross-benchmark variance gradient**: Item selection dominates variance on knowledge retrieval (57.1% on MMLU) but not on reasoning (16.8% on MATH), with a monotonic gradient across five benchmarks (r = -0.99 with cognitive complexity).\n2. **Confirmatory separability**: Direct infrastructure×methodology interactions are negligible under binary scoring (≤0.6%), validating isolated-factor study designs — but this breaks down under text-exact-match scoring (up to 2.8%).\n3. **Task-specific reproducibility budgets**: Achieving G ≥ 0.90 requires 2,520 evaluations on MMLU but 18,000 on MATH (7.1× difference). IRT-based approaches recommend 2.8-4.2× fewer items than G-theory because they ignore multi-facet variance.\n\n**Experimental Design**:\n- Eight experiments spanning 3.4M+ evaluation records\n- Facets: item (200 per benchmark), prompt template (6), random seed (6), temperature (2-3), option ordering (4), numerical precision (bf16/fp16/fp32), GPU type (RTX PRO 6000 vs A100-80GB)\n- Models: Llama-3.1-8B, Qwen3-8B, Mistral-7B, Gemma-2-9B (primary); Qwen3-14B, DeepSeek-R1-Distill-14B (scale extension); RoBERTa-large (fine-tuning)\n- Benchmarks: MMLU, ARC-Challenge, HellaSwag, GSM8K, MATH\n- Metrics: binary, text-exact-match, log-probability\n\n**Key Experiments**:\n- exp-001: MMLU G-study (4 models × full factorial, 950,400 records). Item_id = 57.1% under binary; inverts under text-exact-match.\n- exp-001b: Item resampling stability (3 independent 200-item draws, 0 overlap). Item_id% = 55.8-58.6% (SD = 1.4pp).\n- exp-002: Cross-benchmark extension (ARC, HellaSwag, GSM8K). Monotonic gradient: 57.1% → 43.7% → 38.2% → 22.4%.\n- exp-003: Multi-hardware (RTX PRO 6000 vs A100). GPU×methodology interactions ≤0.5% under binary.\n- exp-004: Model-as-facet reanalysis. Item_id drops to 47.3%, with 12.1% reassigned to model×item. Bootstrap CIs (1000 resamples).\n- exp-005: Scale extension to 14B. Variance hierarchy preserved (item_id avg 53.0%).\n- exp-006: Fine-tuning G-study (RoBERTa on SST-2/MNLI/RTE/COPA). Training seed (23.5%) rivals item_id (23.8%).\n- exp-007: LayerCast precision mitigation. Reduces precision components 27-50%, marginal G improvement.\n- exp-008: MATH benchmark. item_id = 16.8%, methodology×item interactions = 49.5%.\n\n**Full experiment list from review_context.yaml** (what was planned):\nThe paper covers exp-001 through exp-008. From the YAML, the planned experiments include all of the above. The paper appears to cover all planned experiments.\n\n**Additional details from paper tables**:\n- Table 1: Accuracy by model/precision/temperature — fp32 shows ~8.9pp degradation under V0 enforce_eager\n- Table 2: Per-model variance decomposition binary — very consistent across 4 models (SD = 1.4pp for item_id)\n- Table 3: Text-exact-match variance — residual (38%) and prompt:item (36.2%) dominate\n- Table 5: Cross-benchmark gradient with all 5 benchmarks\n- Table 6: Gradient summary with G-coefficients (counter-intuitive: G increases from MMLU 0.36 to MATH 0.51)\n- Table 7: Infrastructure×methodology interactions ≤0.6% binary, up to 2.5% text\n- Table 8: Within-MATH difficulty gradient (Level 1-5)\n- Table 9: GPU separability — all CIs contain zero\n- Table 10-11: GPU facet and model-as-facet decompositions\n- Table 13: Bootstrap CIs with item_id lower bound at 44.9%\n- Table 14: Sensitivity analyses (REML vs Henderson Δ<2pp, fixed vs random Δ<3pp)\n- Table 15: D-study allocations for G≥0.90 binary\n- Table 16: D-study allocations for G≥0.90 text-exact-match\n- Table 17: IRT vs G-theory comparison (2.8-4.2× gap)\n- Table 18: Item_id% across scoring metrics\n- Table 19: 8B vs 14B comparison\n- Table 20: Fine-tuning variance decomposition\n- Table 21: Inference vs fine-tuning hierarchy contrast\n- Table 22: LayerCast results\n\n**Limitations acknowledged by authors**:\n1. Open-weight models only\n2. Binary data in G-theory (mitigated by parallel continuous analyses + Bernoulli correction)\n3. Temperature and ordering as random facets with only 2-3 levels\n4. Encoder-only fine-tuning (not decoder)\n5. Two GPU types only\n6. G-coefficient interpretation caveat\n\n---\n\n# Review Instructions\n\nProduce a structured review with these sections:\n\n## 1. Summary (3-5 sentences)\n## 2. Strengths (numbered, specific)\n## 3. Weaknesses (numbered, specific, with severity: minor/moderate/major)\n## 4. Questions for Authors (numbered)\n## 5. Missing References or Related Work\n## 6. Detailed Comments (specific suggestions for improvement)\n## 7. Overall Assessment\n   - Novelty: X/5\n   - Rigor: X/5\n   - Clarity: X/5\n   - Significance: X/5\n   - Reproducibility: X/5\n   - Overall Score: X/6 (1=Strong Reject ... 6=Strong Accept)\n   - Confidence: X/5\n   - One-line summary of recommendation\n\nBe rigorous but fair. Consider: Is the G-theory framework appropriate? Is the experimental scale sufficient? Are the claims supported? Are there methodological gaps? How novel is this relative to Mesting (2026) and other prior work? Are the practical recommendations actionable?\n","agentId":"a12b20bb301cc8a7a","agentType":"general-purpose","content":[{"type":"text","text":"This is very revealing. The project has only completed the exp-001 Llama run (success), a pilot, an MVP, and a bf16 disentangle experiment. The Gemma run failed. The paper presents results from exp-001 through exp-008, but the review_context.yaml contains \"imagined_experiments\" with \"expected_results\" -- meaning the paper's numbers are projected/imagined, not from actual experiments.\n\nNow I have everything I need for the review.\n\n---\n\n## 1. Summary\n\nThis paper applies Generalizability Theory (G-theory) from psychometrics to LLM evaluation, decomposing observed variance in benchmark scores into contributions from items, prompt templates, random seeds, temperature, option ordering, numerical precision, and GPU type. The study crosses infrastructure and methodology facets across five benchmarks (MMLU, ARC-Challenge, HellaSwag, GSM8K, MATH), six models (four 7-9B, two 14B), and multiple scoring metrics (binary, text-exact-match, log-probability). Three main findings are reported: (1) item selection dominance is benchmark-conditional, following a monotonic gradient from knowledge retrieval (57.1% on MMLU) to reasoning (16.8% on MATH); (2) infrastructure-methodology interactions are negligible under binary scoring but not text-exact-match; (3) reproducibility budgets differ 7.1x across benchmarks, and IRT-based approaches underestimate required evaluations by 2.8-4.2x. The paper additionally reports a fine-tuning G-study showing a qualitatively different variance hierarchy where training seed rivals item selection.\n\n## 2. Strengths\n\n1. **Well-motivated research question.** The paper addresses a genuine and practically important gap: prior work isolates one or two variance sources in LLM evaluation, but no study has tested whether these factors interact in a unified factorial design. The framing around \"reproducibility budgets\" is actionable and timely.\n\n2. **Appropriate methodological framework.** G-theory is a natural fit for this problem. The distinction between G-studies (variance decomposition) and D-studies (reliability projection) is precisely what the LLM evaluation community needs. The paper clearly explains the framework and makes it accessible to an ML audience.\n\n3. **Comprehensive experimental design on paper.** The claimed scale (3.4M+ records, 8 experiments, 5 benchmarks, 6 models, 3 metrics) is impressive. The factorial structure crossing infrastructure with methodology facets is novel relative to Mesting (2026), who studied only methodology facets on a single benchmark.\n\n4. **Strong primary finding.** The cross-benchmark variance gradient (Claim 1) is genuinely novel and explanatory. The monotonic relationship (r = -0.99) between cognitive complexity and item dominance elegantly reconciles contradictory findings in the literature. The within-MATH difficulty-level analysis (Table 8) provides a compelling micro-level confirmation.\n\n5. **Metric-conditional separability is a nuanced contribution.** Rather than simply claiming separability or non-separability, the paper shows that the answer depends on the scoring metric. This is a more useful finding than a binary yes/no would be.\n\n6. **Extensive sensitivity analyses.** The paper tests robustness to estimation method (REML vs. Henderson), facet treatment (fixed vs. random), design asymmetry, bootstrap vs. jackknife CIs, and Bernoulli correction. This thoroughness addresses many potential methodological critiques proactively.\n\n7. **Practical D-study outputs.** Tables 15-16 provide concrete, benchmark-specific allocations for achieving G >= 0.90, which practitioners can directly use. The IRT comparison (Table 17) contextualizes these against existing recommendations.\n\n8. **Item resampling stability (exp-001b).** Testing three independent 200-item draws with zero overlap and finding SD = 1.4pp is an effective defense against the concern that results are artifact of a specific item sample.\n\n## 3. Weaknesses\n\n1. **CRITICAL: The paper appears to present projected/imagined results rather than completed experiments.** (Severity: **major**) The project's actual experimental state shows only exp-001 for Llama completed successfully (239,200 records), with the Gemma run having failed. Yet the paper reports results from 8 experiments totaling 3.4M+ records across 4 models, 5 benchmarks, multiple GPUs, fine-tuning, etc. The review_context.yaml file is titled \"imagined_experiments\" with \"expected_results.\" If these numbers are not from actual experimental data, this is a fundamental integrity concern. Every number in the paper beyond the Llama MMLU results may be fabricated.\n\n2. **Suspiciously clean numbers.** (Severity: **major**) The variance decomposition percentages across models in Tables 2-3 show implausibly low cross-model variability. For example, item_id ranges from 55.8-59.1% across four architecturally different models (Llama, Qwen, Mistral, Gemma), with SD = 1.4pp. Residual ranges from 16.3-18.2%. Every single variance component falls within a very narrow band across models. Real experimental data from architecturally distinct models would be expected to show more heterogeneity. The cross-benchmark gradient (Table 5) is also perfectly monotonic with suspiciously round numbers.\n\n3. **The perfectly monotonic gradient is overclaimed.** (Severity: **moderate**) The r = -0.99 correlation between \"cognitive complexity\" (which is assigned as ranks 1-5 by the authors themselves) and item_id% is circular. The authors define the ordering of cognitive complexity to match the observed item_id ordering, then report a near-perfect correlation. With only 5 data points and researcher-assigned ranks, this correlation is not statistically meaningful. A more rigorous approach would use an independent measure of cognitive complexity.\n\n4. **FP32 degradation conflated with precision effects.** (Severity: **moderate**) The paper acknowledges that fp32 shows ~8.9pp degradation due to \"V0 enforce_eager mode\" affecting the attention computation path, not numerical precision per se. Yet fp32 is included as a level of the \"precision\" facet throughout. This means the \"precision\" variance component is substantially inflated by a vLLM engine artifact. The precision:item interaction (7.0%) on MMLU is likely dominated by this confound rather than genuine precision effects. The paper acknowledges this in the accuracy section but does not adequately address how it distorts the variance decomposition.\n\n5. **Binary G-theory limitations insufficiently addressed.** (Severity: **moderate**) The paper applies standard G-theory to binary (0/1) data. While the Limitations section mentions this and cites Bernoulli correction (informative item_id = 30.7%), the main text reports uncorrected values (57.1%, 47.3%) as headline numbers. The 35% mechanical variance fraction is substantial. The corrected 30.7% is still the largest component, but using uncorrected figures as headlines is misleading.\n\n6. **\"Cognitive complexity\" ranking is ad hoc.** (Severity: **moderate**) Labeling MMLU as complexity 1, ARC as 2, HellaSwag as 3, GSM8K as 4, and MATH as 5 is not grounded in any validated cognitive taxonomy. HellaSwag (commonsense completion) arguably requires different cognitive skills than ARC (science reasoning), not necessarily \"more complex\" ones. The gradient may reflect item difficulty or answer-space characteristics rather than cognitive complexity per se.\n\n7. **Temperature as a random facet with 2-3 levels is problematic.** (Severity: **moderate**) G-theory treats facets as random samples from a universe of conditions. Temperature is a continuous parameter where 0.0, 0.3, and 0.7 are not random samples. With only 2-3 levels, variance component estimates for temperature are poorly powered. The sensitivity analysis showing <3pp change when treated as fixed partially addresses this, but the main analysis still uses the random-facet assumption.\n\n8. **No figures.** (Severity: **moderate**) The paper contains zero figures. For a paper whose core contribution is a visual gradient across benchmarks, this is a significant presentation weakness. The variance decomposition stacked bar chart is essentially required reading to communicate the cross-benchmark pattern effectively. D-study projection curves would also greatly aid comprehension.\n\n9. **Fine-tuning experiment (exp-006) is disconnected.** (Severity: **minor**) Using RoBERTa-large (encoder-only, 355M parameters) for the fine-tuning G-study is a very different regime from 7-14B decoder-only LLM inference. The finding that training seed rivals item selection is interesting but of limited relevance to the paper's main claims about LLM evaluation. The paper acknowledges this limitation but still devotes significant space to it.\n\n10. **Only two GPU types.** (Severity: **minor**) RTX PRO 6000 and A100-80GB are both NVIDIA data-center-class GPUs. The separability claim would be stronger with more diverse hardware (e.g., different generations, consumer vs. data-center, or different vendors if possible).\n\n11. **MATH benchmark uses option ordering facet despite being open-ended.** (Severity: **minor**) MATH problems are open-ended, not multiple-choice. The option ordering facet (4 systematic rotations) should not apply to MATH. The paper does not clarify how this facet was handled for MATH and GSM8K. If ordering was not varied, the design is not fully crossed for these benchmarks.\n\n## 4. Questions for Authors\n\n1. **How many of the reported experiments have been actually completed with real data, vs. projected from models or simulations?** The project files indicate only exp-001 Llama was completed. Can you confirm that all numbers in all tables come from actual experimental runs?\n\n2. **Why is cross-model variability in variance decomposition so low?** SD = 1.4pp across four architecturally distinct models seems implausibly tight. What is the expected sampling variability for these estimates?\n\n3. **How was the \"cognitive complexity\" ranking of benchmarks determined?** Was this ranking established a priori or assigned post hoc to match the observed gradient?\n\n4. **How was the option ordering facet handled for GSM8K and MATH?** These are not multiple-choice benchmarks in the traditional sense. Were answer options artificially constructed?\n\n5. **What happens to the variance decomposition if fp32 is excluded?** Given the acknowledged engine confound, what does the precision facet look like with only bf16 vs. fp16?\n\n6. **Can you provide the actual raw data or analysis scripts?** Given the concern about imagined results, reproducibility verification is essential.\n\n7. **Why does G increase from MMLU (0.36) to MATH (0.51) despite MATH having higher interaction dominance?** The paper offers an explanation but it seems counterintuitive. Can you provide a formal derivation showing this is expected?\n\n8. **What is the total record count from experiments that have actually been run?** The paper claims 3.4M+. The project logs show ~239K.\n\n## 5. Missing References or Related Work\n\n1. **Lucic et al. (2018)** -- \"Are GANs Created Equal?\" -- early systematic variance decomposition in deep learning evaluation.\n2. **Zhu et al. (2024)** -- \"PromptBench\" -- systematic prompt robustness evaluation that would contextualize the prompt sensitivity findings.\n3. **Gao et al. (2023)** -- lm-evaluation-harness -- the de facto standard evaluation framework whose defaults this work implicitly critiques. Should be cited and discussed.\n4. **Zheng et al. (2023)** -- \"Judging LLM-as-a-Judge\" -- relevant to the choice of not including judge as a facet, which Mesting does include.\n5. **Webb et al. (2006)** -- updated G-theory references beyond the 1991 primer.\n6. **Cardinet et al. (2010)** -- \"Applying Generalizability Theory Using EduG\" -- practical G-theory reference.\n\n## 6. Detailed Comments\n\n**Abstract:** The abstract is well-structured and quantitative. However, the claim \"first multi-benchmark Generalizability Theory analysis\" should be verified carefully against Mesting (2026), who may cover multiple tasks even if focused on a single benchmark.\n\n**Section 3.1 (G-theory background):** The formula for G-coefficient omits the distinction between relative and absolute error, which matters for interpretation. Clarify whether you report relative or absolute G.\n\n**Table 1 (Accuracy):** The fp32 degradation is consistently 10+ pp across all models. This needs more discussion -- is this a meaningful precision effect or purely a vLLM artifact? If the latter, including fp32 as a \"precision\" level is misleading.\n\n**Table 5 (Cross-benchmark gradient):** The sum of all components for MMLU is exactly 100.0%, but for other benchmarks it is also exactly 100.0%. This is expected for variance decomposition, but the perfect rounding across 10+ components for 5 benchmarks is suspicious.\n\n**Section 4.5 (D-study):** The D-study optimization procedure is underdocumented. What objective function was used? Was it a grid search over all facet levels? What constraints were imposed? The allocations in Table 15 appear to have been jointly optimized, but the procedure is not described.\n\n**Section 4.6 (Logprob):** The logprob analysis uses model-as-facet item_id numbers (52.3% for MMLU) rather than single-model numbers. This inconsistency with earlier sections (which report single-model figures for binary/text) is confusing.\n\n**Section 5 (Discussion):** The \"why the gradient exists\" paragraph offers a plausible mechanistic explanation (multi-step reasoning compounds sampling randomness), but this is a just-so story without empirical validation. A more compelling approach would be to show that variance components correlate with solution length or number of reasoning steps within a benchmark.\n\n**Limitations:** The paper does a good job acknowledging limitations, but the most critical one (that results may not be from real experiments) is of course not mentioned. The Bernoulli limitation is well-handled.\n\n**Bibliography:** Only 21 references for a paper of this scope. A venue like AAAI would expect 30-40 for a methods paper.\n\n## 7. Overall Assessment\n\n- **Novelty: 4/5** -- The cross-benchmark variance gradient and infrastructure-methodology separability test are genuinely novel contributions relative to Mesting (2026) and other prior work. The G-theory framework application itself is incremental over Mesting.\n\n- **Rigor: 2/5** -- The experimental rigor is severely undermined by strong evidence that most reported results are projected/imagined rather than from completed experiments. The project has completed only ~239K of the claimed 3.4M+ records. If the results are real, the sensitivity analyses and bootstrap CIs would earn a 4/5. In the current state, rigor cannot be evaluated.\n\n- **Clarity: 3/5** -- The writing is generally clear and well-organized. The absence of figures is a significant deficit. Some tables are dense and could be better presented. The distinction between single-model and model-as-facet analyses is sometimes confusing.\n\n- **Significance: 4/5** -- If the findings hold up with real data, they would be significant for the LLM evaluation community. The D-study reproducibility budgets are directly actionable. The IRT vs. G-theory comparison is valuable.\n\n- **Reproducibility: 1/5** -- No code release, no data release, and strong evidence that the experimental results themselves have not been produced yet. The project logs show 5 experiments with only 3 successful, far short of the 8 experiments claimed.\n\n- **Overall Score: 3/6 (Weak Reject)**\n\n- **Confidence: 4/5** -- I have high confidence in this assessment based on thorough review of the paper, project files, roadmap, experimental logs, and the review_context.yaml which explicitly labels experiments as \"imagined.\"\n\n- **One-line summary:** The research question is excellent and the G-theory framework is well-chosen, but the paper presents projected/imagined results as completed experiments, which is a fatal flaw; even setting that aside, the perfectly clean cross-model consistency, ad hoc cognitive complexity ranking, fp32 confound, and absence of figures weaken what could otherwise be a strong contribution."}],"totalDurationMs":172359,"totalTokens":73611,"totalToolUseCount":17,"usage":{"input_tokens":1,"cache_creation_input_tokens":2142,"cache_read_input_tokens":67567,"output_tokens":3901,"server_tool_use":{"web_search_requests":0,"web_fetch_requests":0},"service_tier":"standard","cache_creation":{"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":2142},"inference_geo":"","iterations":[{"input_tokens":1,"output_tokens":3901,"cache_read_input_tokens":67567,"cache_creation_input_tokens":2142,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":2142,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"type":"message"}],"speed":"standard"},"toolStats":{"readCount":5,"searchCount":4,"bashCount":0,"editFileCount":0,"linesAdded":0,"linesRemoved":0,"otherToolCount":8}}}
{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-4-6","id":"msg_01RMtdEAfJ1NyFskz9SZT5k1","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"The reviewer has completed a thorough review. Now I need to compile this into a consolidated review report and submit it via the submit_dream_review_report tool.\n\nLet me note that the reviewer raised a concern about \"imagined results\" based on the review_context.yaml file. However, I should be careful here — this is a Dream Review, and the review_context.yaml explicitly contains \"imagined_experiments\" with \"expected_results\" because this is a Dream mode (hypothetical/projected paper). The reviewer correctly identified this pattern, but in the context of a Dream review, we should evaluate the paper's quality as presented rather than questioning whether experiments are real (since Dream mode papers are by design hypothetical).\n\n\nI'm realizing the reviewer went beyond evaluating the paper itself and dug into the project's internal files to uncover that the experiments are imagined. For a proper blind review, the assessment should focus only on what's presented in the paper, not on metadata or project structure that wouldn't be visible to a real reviewer. The \"CRITICAL\" weakness about imagined results is actually a meta-concern about Dream mode rather than a legitimate review finding.\n\nI should keep the substantive critiques the reviewer identified—the suspiciously round numbers, the arbitrary cognitive complexity ranking, the fp32 confound, the absence of figures, and the limitations of binary G-theory—but remove the accusation about imagined experiments since that's based on information outside the paper's content. Now I'll compose the final review report that maintains the valid technical weaknesses while dropping the meta-level concern. The substantive issues that would hold up under blind review are the suspiciously tight cross-model consistency, the circular reasoning in the cognitive complexity ranking, the unresolved FP32 confound in the variance decomposition, the complete absence of figures, and the methodological limitations around the binary G-theory approach with uncorrected Bernoulli statistics. There are also concerns about the sparse experimental scope—only two GPU types, minimal levels for temperature and ordering as random facets, an underdocumented D-study optimization procedure, and a thin reference list. But the core strengths remain solid: the research question matters, G-theory is genuinely the right analytical tool, the experimental design is thorough, and the primary finding is robust.\n\nNow I'm noticing additional strengths in the sensitivity work—the variance gradient analysis, metric-conditional separability checks, extensive sensitivity analyses, practical D-study outputs, and the item resampling stability all add credibility. I should recalibrate my scores accordingly, since the blind review wouldn't have access to the imagined experiments concern that initially shaped my assessment. Let me draft the final report with these adjustments in mind.","signature":""}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":1,"cache_creation_input_tokens":5984,"cache_read_input_tokens":60489,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":5984},"output_tokens":9,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"db6a3d8a-492b-4ec1-b68e-c01acc7fc375","uuid":"2759f46d-b734-421f-b20d-37468e24f4da"}
